// sfn/test — Process-lifetime control for tests (SFEP-0010 §3.2).
//
// Tests that drive a subprocess have had spawn, capture, and per-child
// env/cwd since `fixtures.sfn`, but nothing that bounds a child in *time*
// or tears one down on demand. That gap pushes such tests onto a shell
// vehicle:
//
//   * A blocking server (`serve()` never returns) is reaped by prepending
//     `timeout <secs>` to the argv, after a `sh -c "command -v timeout"`
//     probe — and the test SKIPS itself when neither `timeout` nor
//     `gtimeout` is on `PATH`. Thirteen e2e files carry a near-verbatim
//     copy of that `_has_cmd` / `_timeout_cmd` / `_spawn_server` trio.
//   * Readiness is polled by shelling `process.run(["sleep", "0.5"])`
//     between probe attempts — a fork+exec per tick, in a hand-rolled
//     bounded retry loop repeated across twelve files.
//
// Both workarounds rest on a premise that is no longer true. Their comments
// say "there is no process-kill primitive"; `process.handle_kill` has
// existed since SFN-402 (SFEP-0050 §5.1), and it is what the test runner
// itself uses for its per-file deadline. The skip-on-missing-`timeout`
// branch therefore silently disables those tests on any host without
// coreutils — a coverage hole, not merely an ergonomic one.
//
// MIGRATION STATUS: this module provides the capability;
// `compiler/tests/e2e/serve_loopback_test.sfn` is the migrated exemplar.
// The other twelve loopback files still carry the shell trio and still
// skip themselves without `timeout`. Do not read the past tense above into
// the call sites — the hole is closed for tests written against this
// module, and remains open in the ones not yet moved.
//
// This module closes the gap entirely inside the capsule. It introduces no
// runtime or compiler surface: every primitive it needs (`spawn_with_env`,
// `handle_kill`, framed stdout/stderr reads, `io.poll_any`) already ships
// as a `process.*` / `io.*` builtin. Nothing here is a seed dependency
// (`.claude/rules/seed-dependency.md`), so it needs no seed cut.
//
// The drain loop below deliberately mirrors `_run_test_binary` in
// `compiler/src/cli/commands/test/exec.sfn` — the runner's own proven
// deadline implementation, including its Windows fallback branch. A capsule
// cannot import a compiler-internal module, so this is a paired copy in the
// same sense as `_pool_managed_keys` in `fixtures.sfn`: when the runner's
// loop changes shape, this one should follow.
//
// Raw `process.*` builtins are called directly rather than through
// `sfn/os`'s typed `ProcessHandle`. `sfn/os` wraps a chunked stdout read
// into an `OwnedBuf` the caller must free and offers no stderr counterpart,
// no `handle_kill`, and no fd accessors — so routing through it would mean
// widening that capsule and then unwrapping buffers back into strings.
// `compile_assert.sfn` already calls `process.run_capture` directly for the
// same reason.
import { int_to_string } from "sfn/strings";

// The result of running a child to completion under a deadline.
//
// `timed_out` is a first-class field rather than something a caller infers
// from `exit`, because it cannot be inferred: `handle_wait` after a SIGKILL
// returns `128 + 9 = 137`, which is indistinguishable from a child the
// kernel OOM-killed on its own. The runtime's `handle_kill` comment
// (`runtime/sfn/process.sfn`) states the rule outright — "`137` must never
// be the timeout discriminator (the caller tracks a 'killed on deadline'
// flag instead)". The runner remaps its own deadline kills to `124` for the
// same reason; a boolean is the honest form of that signal, so `exit` here
// is always the code the child actually died with.
//
// `stdout` / `stderr` hold everything drained before the child exited or
// was killed. A killed child's partial output is kept, not discarded — it
// is usually the only evidence of where the child hung.
struct ChildOutcome {
    exit: int;
    stdout: string;
    stderr: string;
    timed_out: boolean;
}

// A child spawned to run concurrently with the test that started it —
// typically a blocking server the test then drives over a socket.
//
// `handle_id` is `0` when the spawn itself failed; `stop_background` and
// the scoped `with_background` both tolerate that, so a call site does not
// have to branch before tearing down. `label` is carried only for
// diagnostics.
//
// This is a plain copyable struct holding a raw handle id, NOT an owning
// handle: copying it does not duplicate the child, and stopping one copy
// leaves every other copy dangling. Exactly one `stop_background` per
// `start_background` — see the warning there.
struct Background {
    handle_id: int;
    label: string;
}

// Chunk size for a single framed read. Matches the runner's own 64 KiB
// read window in `exec.sfn` so the two loops behave alike under a chatty
// child.
let _READ_CHUNK: int = 65536;

// How many consecutive `io.poll_any` failures to tolerate before giving up
// on a child. `io.poll_any` returns `-1` for a timeout, but also for an
// allocation failure or a non-retryable `poll(2)` errno — and those are
// sticky, not transient. Without a bound, a `deadline_ms <= 0` drain would
// spin at 100% CPU forever on one, since there is no deadline to break it.
// `exec.sfn` needs no such guard because the runner always passes a
// deadline; this module documents no-deadline as a supported mode, which
// makes the hazard reachable (`.claude/rules/code-style.md` asks for a
// guard counter on input-driven loops).
let _MAX_POLL_FAILURES: int = 1024;

// Drain a live child's stdout and stderr until both reach EOF, or until
// `deadline_ms` elapses — then reap it.
//
// Returns the child's outcome. `deadline_ms <= 0` means "no deadline":
// drain until EOF, however long that takes.
//
// The loop demultiplexes the two pipes with `io.poll_any` over the raw fds
// and reads ONLY the fd poll reports ready. The chunk reads are blocking
// `read(2)` mirrors, so reading a not-ready fd would deadlock. The fds go
// to `-1` at EOF, so they are re-fetched every iteration rather than
// hoisted.
//
// `handle_wait` is the sole reaper/free path and is ALWAYS called, on every
// exit from the loop including the deadline path — skipping it leaks the
// handle plus both stash buffers and leaves a zombie.
fn _drain_bounded(handle_id: int, deadline_ms: int) -> ChildOutcome ![clock, io] {
    let mut out_buf: string = "";
    let mut err_buf: string = "";
    let mut timed_out: boolean = false;
    let mut poll_failures: int = 0;
    let start_ms: int = monotonic_millis();
    loop {
        if process.handle_stdout_at_eof(handle_id) && process.handle_stderr_at_eof(handle_id) {
            break;
        }
        let mut poll_to: int = -1;
        if deadline_ms > 0 {
            let remaining: int = deadline_ms - ((monotonic_millis() - start_ms) as int);
            if remaining <= 0 {
                process.handle_kill(handle_id);
                timed_out = true;
                break;
            }
            poll_to = remaining;
        }
        let out_fd = process.handle_stdout_fd(handle_id);
        let err_fd = process.handle_stderr_fd(handle_id);
        let mut fds: int[] = [];
        if out_fd >= 0 { fds.push(out_fd); }
        if err_fd >= 0 { fds.push(err_fd); }
        let mut read_out: boolean = false;
        let mut read_err: boolean = false;
        if fds.length == 0 {
            // No fd to select on — that is Windows, where the accessors are
            // `-1` by design and `io.poll_any` is a `-1` stub. Drive the
            // framed reads directly, as `platform/process_windows.sfn`
            // prescribes: each pumps both pipes, so neither stream can wedge
            // the other, and each blocks until a byte or EOF, which is what
            // keeps this from spinning. Termination stays the both-at-EOF
            // check above. The deadline is therefore only observed between
            // reads on that platform — an untimed framed read cannot be
            // interrupted, so a wholly silent hung child is not killed here.
            // POSIX cannot reach this branch: a read only clears an fd to
            // `-1` when it drained nothing, so both fds are `-1` only once
            // both stashes are empty, which the both-at-EOF check above has
            // already broken on.
            read_out = !process.handle_stdout_at_eof(handle_id);
            read_err = !process.handle_stderr_at_eof(handle_id);
        } else {
            let ready = io.poll_any(fds, poll_to);
            if ready < 0 {
                if deadline_ms > 0 && ((monotonic_millis() - start_ms) as int) >= deadline_ms {
                    process.handle_kill(handle_id);
                    timed_out = true;
                    break;
                }
                // A sticky poll failure (allocation failure, non-retryable
                // errno) would otherwise spin forever here when no deadline
                // bounds the drain. Give up rather than burn a core.
                poll_failures = poll_failures + 1;
                if poll_failures >= _MAX_POLL_FAILURES {
                    process.handle_kill(handle_id);
                    break;
                }
                continue;
            }
            poll_failures = 0;
            read_out = ready == out_fd;
            read_err = ready == err_fd;
        }
        if read_out {
            let chunk = process.handle_read_bytes_stdout(handle_id, _READ_CHUNK);
            if chunk.length > 0 { out_buf = out_buf + chunk; }
        }
        if read_err {
            let chunk = process.handle_read_bytes_stderr(handle_id, _READ_CHUNK);
            if chunk.length > 0 { err_buf = err_buf + chunk; }
        }
    }
    let rc = process.handle_wait(handle_id);
    return ChildOutcome {
        exit: rc,
        stdout: out_buf,
        stderr: err_buf,
        timed_out: timed_out
    };
}

// The outcome handed back when the spawn itself never happened. `127`
// mirrors `process.run`'s missing-command code, matching what the runner
// reports for the same failure.
fn _spawn_failed() -> ChildOutcome {
    return ChildOutcome {
        exit: 127,
        stdout: "",
        stderr: "",
        timed_out: false
    };
}

// Run `argv` to completion under a wall-clock deadline, capturing both
// streams. This is the bounded replacement for a `timeout <secs> ...` argv
// vehicle: no external `timeout`/`gtimeout` binary, so no `command -v`
// probe and no skip-when-absent branch.
//
// `env` is a flat `"KEY=VALUE"` array with the same contract as
// `process.run_capture` — an EMPTY array means an empty child environment,
// not an inherited one. A child that needs `PATH`/`HOME`/`TMPDIR` must be
// given them explicitly; for a nested `sfn test`/`build`/`run`, build the
// array with `clean_runner_env(nested_runner_scratch(label))`.
//
// `deadline_ms <= 0` waits indefinitely. On expiry the child is SIGKILLed,
// still reaped, and the returned outcome has `timed_out == true` with
// whatever output was drained before the kill.
//
// The child's stdin is closed immediately, giving it `< /dev/null`
// semantics — see `run_bounded_in`. Use `run_bounded_stdin` to feed it.
fn run_bounded(argv: string[], env: string[], deadline_ms: int) -> ChildOutcome ![clock, io] {
    return run_bounded_in(argv, env, "", deadline_ms);
}

// `run_bounded` with a per-child working directory. An empty `cwd`
// inherits the parent's, identical to `run_bounded`.
//
// This is the deadline-aware sibling of `with_cwd`, and it retires the
// `bash -c "cd <dir> && ..."` vehicle that `docs/conventions/e2e-tests.md`
// used to prescribe: the directory is applied to the single spawned child
// via `spawn_with_env_cwd`, never through a process-global `chdir(2)`,
// so concurrent tests under `--jobs N` are unaffected.
//
// Stdin is closed before the drain starts. `spawn_with_env_cwd` ALWAYS
// creates a stdin pipe and hands the child its read end, and the parent
// holds the write end until `handle_wait` — so without this close, any
// child that reads stdin (`sort`, `cat`, a tool that is not on a tty)
// would block on a pipe that never reaches EOF. With `deadline_ms <= 0`
// that is a permanent hang. Closing here restores the `< /dev/null`
// semantics of both idioms this replaces: `process.run_capture` creates no
// stdin pipe at all, and a `timeout <secs> <cmd>` vehicle inherits the
// parent's stdin.
fn run_bounded_in(argv: string[], env: string[], cwd: string, deadline_ms: int) -> ChildOutcome ![clock, io] {
    let handle_id = process.spawn_with_env_cwd(argv, env, cwd);
    if handle_id == 0 { return _spawn_failed(); }
    process.handle_close_stdin(handle_id);
    return _drain_bounded(handle_id, deadline_ms);
}

// `run_bounded` that first writes `input` to the child's stdin and closes
// it, so a child reading to EOF terminates on its own.
//
// `process.run_capture` cannot pipe stdin at all, which is why tests that
// drive an interactive prompt hand-composed a write / close / drain / wait
// sequence against the raw handle builtins. Stdin is closed before the
// drain begins so a child reading to EOF terminates on its own.
//
// TWO LIMITS the caller must respect, because the write happens BEFORE the
// drain loop and therefore before the deadline clock starts — neither can
// be enforced from here:
//
//   * `input` must fit the pipe buffer (64 KiB on Linux) unless the child
//     drains promptly. A larger payload against a child that writes output
//     before reading blocks the parent in `handle_write` with no deadline
//     to break it. Feed a bigger payload through a file instead.
//   * If the child exits before reading, the write hits a closed pipe and
//     raises SIGPIPE. Nothing in the runtime installs `SIG_IGN` for it, so
//     that terminates the TEST PROCESS with exit 141 — not just this call.
//     Only pass `input` to a child known to read it; `run_bounded` is the
//     right call for one that does not.
//
// The exact-length `handle_write_bytes` is used rather than
// `handle_write`, which `strlen`s its argument and would silently truncate
// input at the first embedded NUL.
fn run_bounded_stdin(argv: string[], env: string[], input: string, deadline_ms: int) -> ChildOutcome ![clock, io] {
    let handle_id = process.spawn_with_env(argv, env);
    if handle_id == 0 { return _spawn_failed(); }
    if input.length > 0 {
        process.handle_write_bytes(handle_id, input, input.length);
    }
    process.handle_close_stdin(handle_id);
    return _drain_bounded(handle_id, deadline_ms);
}

// Spawn `argv` and return immediately, leaving it running alongside the
// test. The caller MUST hand the result to `stop_background` — prefer the
// scoped `with_background`, which cannot forget.
//
// Output is not drained while the child runs. A background child that
// writes more than a pipe buffer (~64 KiB) before being stopped will block
// on that write; a server whose per-request logging is that chatty should
// be spawned with its output redirected, or driven with `run_bounded`
// instead.
//
// Stdin is closed immediately, for the reason spelled out in
// `run_bounded_in`: the spawn always creates a stdin pipe, and a
// background child that reads stdin would otherwise block on one that
// never reaches EOF.
fn start_background(argv: string[], env: string[], cwd: string, label: string) -> Background ![io] {
    let handle_id = process.spawn_with_env_cwd(argv, env, cwd);
    if handle_id != 0 { process.handle_close_stdin(handle_id); }
    return Background { handle_id: handle_id, label: label };
}

// Kill a background child and reap it, returning what it produced.
//
// CONSUMES the handle — call it EXACTLY ONCE per `start_background`.
// `handle_wait` is the free path, so a second call dereferences freed
// memory in `handle_kill` and then double-frees. `Background` is a plain
// copyable struct carrying a raw handle id, so a stale copy cannot be
// invalidated for you; in particular, do NOT call this from inside a
// `with_background` body — that fixture stops the child itself on scope
// exit, and the two stops would collide.
//
// Safe to call on a failed spawn (`handle_id == 0`) and on a child that has
// already exited on its own: `handle_kill` returning `-1` for a dead pid is
// explicitly not fatal, and the follow-up drain simply sees both pipes at
// EOF. `timed_out` is always `false` — the stop was the caller's decision,
// not a deadline expiring. `exit` is reported as the child actually died,
// so a SIGKILLed server reads `137`.
//
// The post-kill drain is bounded so a wedged grandchild holding the pipes
// open cannot hang teardown. A killed child's pipes normally hit EOF at
// once, so this budget is a backstop, not a wait — but it does mean
// `stdout`/`stderr` may be TRUNCATED if a surviving grandchild holds them
// open past the budget, and the returned outcome cannot distinguish that
// from a complete capture.
fn stop_background(bg: Background) -> ChildOutcome ![clock, io] {
    if bg.handle_id == 0 { return _spawn_failed(); }
    process.handle_kill(bg.handle_id);
    let outcome = _drain_bounded(bg.handle_id, 5000);
    return ChildOutcome {
        exit: outcome.exit,
        stdout: outcome.stdout,
        stderr: outcome.stderr,
        timed_out: false
    };
}

// Run a background child for the duration of `body`, and kill + reap it on
// scope exit — including when `body` throws. Returns whatever `body`
// returns.
//
// This is the structured-lifetime fixture for a blocking server, and the
// direct replacement for the `_spawn_server` + external-`timeout` idiom: a
// test can no longer orphan a child by returning early or failing an
// assertion, so it no longer needs a wall-clock timeout as its only
// teardown mechanism, and no longer needs to skip itself when that binary
// is absent.
//
// `body` receives the live `Background` (so it can report `label` or check
// `handle_id != 0`) and follows the same shape as `with_tmp_dir`'s: an
// ordinary closure over the enclosing test's locals, typed with no `![io]`
// row because the effect checker attributes a lambda body's effects to the
// scope that defines it. `-> int` is the interim return shape shared with
// the other scoped fixtures.
//
// `body` must NOT call `stop_background` on the handle it is given: this
// fixture stops it on scope exit, and a second stop is a use-after-free.
// A body that wants the server's output should capture it another way
// (redirect the child, or drive it with `run_bounded`).
//
// SFN-843: a `body` (or any lambda) must not capture an enclosing `let`
// that has no type annotation and whose initializer is a call — that
// mistypes the capture's env slot as `i8*`, which fails to build for
// scalars and SILENTLY yields zero for structs, while passing `sfn check`.
// Annotating the captured `let` avoids it, as does reaching the value
// through a helper function. Delete this note when SFN-843 lands.
fn with_background(argv: string[], env: string[], cwd: string, label: string, body: fn (Background) -> int) -> int ![clock, io] {
    let bg = start_background(argv, env, cwd, label);
    try {
        let result = body(bg);
        let _ = stop_background(bg);
        return result;
    } catch (err) {
        // Reap first, then re-throw, so the failing test still fails with
        // its own message rather than a teardown error — and so a thrown
        // assertion cannot leak the child.
        let _ = stop_background(bg);
        throw err;
    }
}

// Poll `probe` until it returns true, or until `deadline_ms` elapses.
// Returns whether it became true in time.
//
// This retires the hand-rolled readiness loop that shelled
// `process.run(["sleep", "0.5"])` between attempts — a whole fork+exec per
// tick. The prelude's `sleep(ms)` does the same wait in-process, so a poll
// costs nothing but the probe.
//
// The budget is wall-clock rather than an attempt count. An attempt count
// (`90` tries at `0.5s`) silently changes meaning when the probe itself
// gets slower, which is exactly what happens on a loaded CI runner; a
// deadline does not.
//
// `probe` carries no effect row, for the same reason the scoped fixtures'
// bodies do not: its effects are attributed to the scope that defines it.
//
// SFN-843: a probe must not capture an enclosing `let` that has no type
// annotation and whose initializer is a CALL. That mistypes the capture's
// env slot as `i8*` — a build failure for scalars, and a SILENT zero for
// structs — while `sfn check` stays green. Annotating the captured `let`
// (`let port: int = _lb_port();`) avoids it, as does reaching the value
// through a helper function, which is what the in-tree probes do. Delete
// this note when SFN-843 lands.
fn wait_until(probe: fn () -> boolean, deadline_ms: int, interval_ms: int) -> boolean ![clock, io] {
    let start_ms = monotonic_millis();
    let mut wait_ms = interval_ms;
    if wait_ms <= 0 { wait_ms = 50; }
    loop {
        if probe() { return true; }
        let elapsed: int = (monotonic_millis() - start_ms) as int;
        if elapsed >= deadline_ms { return false; }
        let remaining: int = deadline_ms - elapsed;
        let mut nap = wait_ms;
        if nap > remaining { nap = remaining; }
        sleep(nap);
    }
    return false;
}

// Render a `ChildOutcome` as a one-line diagnostic suffix, so a failing
// assertion can say why without the call site formatting three fields.
// Distinguishes a deadline kill from an ordinary non-zero exit, which the
// exit code alone cannot.
fn describe_outcome(outcome: ChildOutcome) -> string {
    if outcome.timed_out {
        return "timed out (killed on deadline, exit " + int_to_string(outcome.exit)
            + ")";
    }
    return "exit " + int_to_string(outcome.exit);
}
